{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 51,
  "title": "Counting on",
  "description": "Count from zero to fourteen without stopping.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Unit 5 taught the shape of Spanish numbers. This unit fills in the ones it skipped: zero, and four through fourteen. After it there is no small number you cannot say.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Zero and four to nine",
        "body": "Cero is nothing at all. Cuatro, seis, siete, ocho and nueve work exactly like the numbers you know: they go in front of the noun and never change. Cinco was in unit 5; these sit on either side of it.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "cero euros",
            "en": "zero euros"
          },
          {
            "es": "siete días",
            "en": "seven days"
          },
          {
            "es": "nueve noches",
            "en": "nine nights"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Eleven to fourteen",
        "body": "Once, doce, trece and catorce are each one word, like the tens. Diez was in unit 5 and quince comes in the next unit; these four close the gap between them. Quince keeps its accent; the others need none.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "once personas",
            "en": "eleven people"
          },
          {
            "es": "doce meses",
            "en": "twelve months"
          },
          {
            "es": "catorce sillas",
            "en": "fourteen chairs"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
